### Steps to Adapt FEM for a Triangular Domain

1. **Create a Mesh**: Generate a mesh of triangular elements within the domain.
2. **Define Basis Functions**: Use basis functions suitable for triangular elements.
3. **Assemble Stiffness Matrix and Load Vector**: Integrate over each triangular element to assemble the stiffness matrix and load vector.
4. **Apply Boundary Conditions**: Apply the boundary conditions to the assembled system.
5. **Solve the System**: Solve the resulting linear system to obtain the potential values at each node.

### MATLAB Code for Triangular Domain

Here is a MATLAB script to solve the Laplace equation \(\nabla^2 u = 0\) for a triangular domain with vertices at \((0, 0)\), \((3, 0)\), and \((0, 1)\):

```matlab
clear;
clc;

% Define the domain vertices
vertices = [0 0; 3 0; 0 1];

% Define the boundary conditions
boundary_conditions = @(x, y) 100 * sin(pi * x / 3);

% Create a mesh of triangular elements
[p, t] = initmesh(vertices, 'Hmax', 0.1);

% Number of nodes
Nnodes = size(p, 1);

% Initialize the stiffness matrix and load vector
K = sparse(Nnodes, Nnodes);
F = zeros(Nnodes, 1);

% Loop over each triangle
for i = 1:size(t, 1)
    % Extract the vertices of the current triangle
    p1 = p(t(i, 1), :);
    p2 = p(t(i, 2), :);
    p3 = p(t(i, 3), :);

    % Compute the area of the triangle
    area = 0.5 * abs(det([p1(1) p1(2) 1; p2(1) p2(2) 1; p3(1) p3(2) 1]));

    % Compute the Jacobian matrix
    J = [p2(1) - p1(1), p3(1) - p1(1); p2(2) - p1(2), p3(2) - p1(2)];

    % Compute the inverse of the Jacobian matrix
    J_inv = inv(J);

    % Compute the basis functions and their derivatives
    N = @(xi, eta) [eta, xi, 1 - xi - eta];
    dN = @(xi, eta) [0, 1, -1; 1, 0, -1] * J_inv;

    % Integrate over the reference triangle
    for xi = 0:0.1:1
        for eta = 0:0.1:1 - xi
            if xi + eta <= 1
                % Compute the basis functions and their derivatives at (xi, eta)
                N_val = N(xi, eta);
                dN_val = dN(xi, eta);

                % Compute the contributions to the stiffness matrix
                K(t(i, :), t(i, :)) = K(t(i, :), t(i, :)) + area * 0.1 * 0.1 * dN_val' * dN_val;

                % Compute the contributions to the load vector
                F(t(i, :)) = F(t(i, :)) + area * 0.1 * 0.1 * N_val' * boundary_conditions(p1(1) + (p2(1) - p1(1)) * xi + (p3(1) - p1(1)) * eta, p1(2) + (p2(2) - p1(2)) * xi + (p3(2) - p1(2)) * eta);
            end
        end
    end
end

% Apply boundary conditions
boundary_nodes = find(p(:, 2) == 0 | p(:, 1) == 0 | p(:, 1) == 3);
K(boundary_nodes, :) = 0;
K(:, boundary_nodes) = 0;
K(boundary_nodes, boundary_nodes) = eye(length(boundary_nodes));
F(boundary_nodes) = 0;

% Solve the system
U = K \ F;

% Plot the solution
figure;
trisurf(t, p(:, 1), p(:, 2), U, 'FaceColor', 'interp');
shading interp;
colorbar;
xlabel('x (m)');
ylabel('y (m)');
zlabel('Potential (V)');
title('Potential Distribution in a Triangular Domain');

% Check the value at (1.5, 0.5)
u_at_1_5_0_5 = interp2(p(:, 1), p(:, 2), U, 1.5, 0.5);
disp(['u(1.5, 0.5) = ', num2str(u_at_1_5_0_5)]);
```

