Certainly! Adjusting the learning rate dynamically in gradient descent can help improve convergence and avoid overshooting the minimum. Here's how you can implement dynamic learning rate adjustment in the context of minimizing the cost functional \( J[u] \) for the heat equation \( u_t = 0.22 u_{xx} \).

### Dynamic Learning Rate Adjustment

Dynamic learning rate adjustment typically involves decreasing the learning rate as the optimization progresses. One common strategy is to reduce the learning rate based on the number of iterations or the change in the cost functional.

### Steps to Implement Dynamic Learning Rate

1. **Initialize Parameters**:
   - Set an initial learning rate \( \eta_0 \).
   - Define a decay factor \( \gamma \) to reduce the learning rate over time.

2. **Gradient Descent Loop**:
   - Compute the gradient of the cost functional \( J[u] \).
   - Update the control parameter \( T \) using the gradient descent update rule.
   - Adjust the learning rate based on the iteration count or the change in the cost functional.

### Example MATLAB Code

Here is an example MATLAB code that includes dynamic learning rate adjustment:

```matlab
% Define the length of the string and time interval
L = 1; % Length of the string
T = 1; % Total time

% Define the grid spacing
dx = 0.01; % Spatial step
dt = 0.005; % Time step

% Define the number of grid points
Nx = L / dx + 1;
Nt = T / dt + 1;

% Initialize the solution matrix
u = zeros(Nx, Nt);

% Define the initial condition
f = @(x) sin(pi * x); % Example initial condition

% Compute the initial displacement
u(:, 1) = f((0:Nx-1)' * dx);

% Define the diffusion coefficient
k = 0.22;

% Define the Courant number
Courant = k * dt / dx^2;

% Time-stepping loop
for n = 1:Nt-1
    for i = 2:Nx-1
        u(i, n+1) = u(i, n) + Courant * (u(i+1, n) - 2 * u(i, n) + u(i-1, n));
    end
end

% Define the initial learning rate and decay factor
eta_0 = 0.01;
gamma = 0.99;

% Maximum number of iterations
max_iter = 10000;

% Tolerance for convergence
tolerance = 1e-6;

% Initialize the control parameter T
T = 20.5; % Close to the analytical minimum

% Initialize the cost functional
J = zeros(1, max_iter);

% Gradient descent loop
for iter = 1:max_iter
    % Compute the current solution with the current T
    u_T = u + (T - u(end,end)) * ones(size(u));

    % Enforce the boundary condition at x=0
    u_T(:, 1) = T;

    % Compute the derivatives
    u_x = gradient(u_T, dx);

    % Compute the cost functional
    J(iter) = trapz(dx, u_x.^2 + 0.5 * u_T.^2);

    % Compute the gradient of the cost functional with respect to T
    % This requires differentiating the cost functional with respect to T
    % For simplicity, we approximate the gradient numerically
    epsilon = 1e-6;
    T_plus = T + epsilon;
    u_T_plus = u + (T_plus - u(end,end)) * ones(size(u));
    u_T_plus(:, 1) = T_plus;
    J_plus = trapz(dx, gradient(u_T_plus, dx).^2 + 0.5 * u_T_plus.^2);
    gradient_J = (J_plus - J(iter)) / epsilon;

    % Update T using gradient descent
    eta = eta_0 * gamma^(iter - 1); % Dynamic learning rate
    T_new = T - eta * gradient_J;

    % Check for convergence
    if abs(T_new - T) < tolerance
        break;
    end

    % Update T
    T = T_new;
end

% Compute the final cost functional
u_final = u + (T - u(end,end)) * ones(size(u));
u_final(:, 1) = T;
u_x_final = gradient(u_final, dx);
J_final = trapz(dx, u_x_final.^2 + 0.5 * u_final.^2);

% Display results
disp(['Initial Cost J[u]: ', num2str(J(1))]);
disp(['Final Cost J[u]: ', num2str(J_final)]);
disp(['Optimal Boundary Condition T: ', num2str(T)]);

% Plot the solution
figure;
surf((0:Nx-1)' * dx, (0:Nt-1)' * dt, u_final);
xlabel('Position (x)');
ylabel('Time (t)');
zlabel('Temperature (u)');
title('Temperature Distribution Over Time');
colorbar;

