The Crank-Nicolson method is a good choice for solving the heat equation due to its stability and accuracy. It is an implicit method that averages the forward and backward Euler methods.

#### 4. Implement the Crank-Nicolson Method

Here's how you can implement the Crank-Nicolson method in MATLAB:

```matlab
% Parameters
L = 1; % Length of the rod
T_total = 25 / 60; % Total time in hours
Nx = 100; % Number of spatial points
Nt = 100; % Number of time steps
dx = L / (Nx - 1); % Spatial step
dt = T_total / Nt; % Time step
alpha = 0.33; % Thermal diffusivity

% Initialize temperature array
u = zeros(Nx, Nt+1);
x = linspace(0, L, Nx);

% Initial condition
u(:, 1) =  20 + 10 * sin(pi * x / L);

% Boundary condition
T = 22; % Initial boundary condition

% Crank-Nicolson matrices
r = alpha * dt / (2 * dx^2);
A = spdiags([ones(Nx-1, 1) -2*ones(Nx, 1) ones(Nx-1, 1)], [-1 0 1], Nx, Nx);
B = spdiags([ones(Nx-1, 1) 2*ones(Nx, 1) -ones(Nx-1, 1)], [-1 0 1], Nx, Nx);

% Solve heat equation using Crank-Nicolson method
for n = 1:Nt
    b = u(:, n) + r * (A * u(:, n) + B * u(:, n));
    u(:, n+1) = A \ b;
    u(1, n+1) = T;
    u(end, n+1) = T;
end

% Compute cost functional
J = compute_cost_functional(u, dx);

disp(['Cost functional J[u] is ', num2str(J)]);

% Helper function to compute cost functional
function J = compute_cost_functional(u, dx)
    J = sum(diff(u, 1, 1).^2 / dx + u.^2) * dx;
end
```

